.TH SWATCH 1 "2026-07-26" "swatch 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
swatch \- switch the whole desktop to a theme
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B swatch
[\fIcommand\fR] [\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Applies a palette to every themed surface on the machine: terminal, editors,
status bar, file manager, browser and wallpaper. A theme is a directory holding
a \fIpalette.toml\fR and a \fIwallpapers/\fR pool, kept under
\fI~/.config/swatch/themes/\fR.
.PP
Swatch writes theme files and one pointer line per surface; it never rewrites a
whole config. Where an app has no theme-file mechanism, swatch replaces only the
block between
.B "# swatch:start"
and
.B "# swatch:end"
markers. A pointer edit that does not match exactly one line is an error, not a
guess.
.PP
Swatch refuses to apply a palette containing the string TODO.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list [\fItheme\fR]
Show available themes, one per line, with the size of each wallpaper pool.
Unfinished scaffolds are listed as such. Given a theme, show that theme's
wallpapers numbered, which are the numbers
.B \-\-pick
takes.
.TP
.B use \fItheme\fR [\fB\-\-pick\fR \fIn\fR|\fIname\fR]
Apply a theme to every surface. Surfaces whose app is not set up on this
machine are skipped, not failed.
.IP
A theme holding more than one wallpaper opens an
.BR fzf (1)
menu, previewed with
.BR chafa (1)
when it is installed, before any surface is written; escaping it changes
nothing. With no terminal, no fzf, or
.BR \-\-pick ,
no menu opens: swatch keeps the wallpaper already on screen if it belongs to
the theme, and otherwise takes the first.
.TP
.B status [\fItheme\fR]
Run
.B use
with the writes turned off and report which files would change. Defaults to the
first theme.
.TP
.B new \fIname\fR \fIimage\fR...
Scaffold a theme directory: copies the images into
.IR wallpapers/ ,
samples the first one's luminance range for the structural roles, seeds the
ANSI 16, and marks the accent TODO. Extraction recovers structure, never
identity.
.TP
.B add \fItheme\fR \fIimage\fR...
Copy more wallpapers into an existing theme, keeping their filenames. A name
already in the pool is an error. Given
.B \-
instead of paths, read them from standard input one per line, so an
interactive selection can be piped in:
.IP
.EX
fzf \-m | swatch add nord \-
.EE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SWATCH_THEMES
Directory holding your themes, one subdirectory each. Defaults to
\fI~/.config/swatch/themes\fR. Point it at a checkout to keep your themes in a
repository of their own.
.SH NOTES
Cider rewrites its own settings file from memory whenever it saves, so a theme
written while Cider is running is reverted with no error. Swatch says so when it
sees Cider running: quit and reopen it, then
.B swatch status
confirms.
.PP
VS Code and Zen only pick up a new theme after the app restarts. Everything
else reloads on its own: sketchybar and borders are reconfigured in place, and
the terminal tools pick up their theme file the next time they run.
.PP
Each macOS Space keeps its own wallpaper, and System Events has no per-Space
API: its "desktop" is a display. Swatch therefore walks the Spaces with
.BR yabai (1),
setting each and restoring focus afterwards. Without yabai it sets the focused
Space only and says so.
.PP
Three surfaces are opt-in, because none of these apps keeps a config file whose
presence would mean "theme me". Swatch writes
\fI~/.config/fzf/colors.sh\fR only when
\fI~/.config/fzf\fR exists,
\fI~/.config/git/swatch.gitconfig\fR only when a gitconfig already includes it, and
\fIswatch\-web.css\fR in Zen's chrome directory only when
\fIuserContent.css\fR imports it:
.IP
.EX
mkdir \-p ~/.config/fzf
# in your shell rc: source ~/.config/fzf/colors.sh
git config \-\-global include.path '~/.config/git/swatch.gitconfig'
# in Zen's chrome/userContent.css: @import "swatch\-web.css";
.EE
.PP
The fzf colours reach a shell the next time it starts, not the one that ran
.BR swatch (1).
.PP
On macOS 26 and later swatch also tints every app icon with the palette accent,
by writing the icon appearance keys in NSGlobalDomain and then asking SkyLight to
apply them, which is what makes the Dock re-render. No icon file is touched, and
.B "System Settings > Appearance"
puts it back. Earlier releases have no such setting and the surface is skipped.
.PP
The system highlight colour behind selected text is set the same way, through
\fBAppleHighlightColor\fR, which takes an arbitrary colour \(em unlike
\fBAppleAccentColor\fR, an eight-value enum with no custom slot, which swatch
leaves alone. Running apps keep the old colour until they are relaunched.
.PP
Dynamic wallpapers are HEIC files carrying \fIapple_desktop:solar\fR or
\fI:h24\fR, often named \fI.jpg\fR.
.B swatch new
takes the extension from
.BR sips (1)
and warns that one palette cannot follow a wallpaper that changes all day.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dt (1),
.BR sips (1)
